The above csharp code generates an Assert statement "Debug.Assert(arrList.Count == New_Ret);".--------------------Here are the assert statements corresponding to the comments that start with "@@@" in the provided C# code:

1. `// @@@ last position of x in new array is lesser than or equal to old array size`
   This comment suggests that we need to assert that the last index of the newly added element x in the array is less than or equal to the old size of the array. We can achieve this by comparing the last index of x in the new array (`New_arrListLastIndexOfX`) to the old size of the array (`Old_arrListCount`).
   Here's the corresponding Assert statement:
   ```cs
   Assert.LessThanOrEqual(New_arrListLastIndexOfX, Old_arrListCount);
   ```

2. `// @@@ new array is either the same or bigger than old array`
   This comment suggests that we need to assert that the new array either contains the same elements or more elements than the old array. We can achieve this by comparing the new array count (`New_arrListCount`) to the old array count (`Old_arrListCount`).
   Here's the corresponding Assert statement:
   ```cs
   Assert.GreaterThanOrEqual(New_arrListCount, Old_arrListCount);
   ```

3. `// @@@ x is removed from the array; therefore, new count of array is either equal to old count - 1 or less`
   This comment suggests that we need to assert that after removing element x from the array, the new array's count is either equal to the old count minus one or less. We can achieve this by comparing the new array count (`New_arrListCount`) to the old array count minus one (`Old_arrListCount - 1`).
   Here's the corresponding Assert statement:
   ```cs
   Assert.LessThanOrEqual(New_arrListCount, Old_arrListCount - 1);
   ```

4. `// @@@ x is added to index in new array is less than or equal to old index or (old index + 1, when x is added to old array)`
   This comment suggests that we need to assert that the index of the newly added element x in the array is either less than or equal to the old index or equal to the old index plus one if the x is added to the old array. We can achieve this by comparing the index of x in the new array (`New_arrListIndexOfX`) to the old index (`Old_arrListIndexOfX`) and also checking for the condition when x is added to the old array.
   Here's the corresponding Assert statement:
